{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A decentralized network is more resilient to correlated failure than a single large service if and only if the distribution of operator-level causal factors (deployment philosophy, personnel, infrastructure vendor, library dependencies) across the network has strictly lower entropy than the equivalent distribution within the large service.","counterconditions":["If the large service has lower operator-level causal entropy (e.g., via internal federation of independent sub-teams) than the decentralized network.","If the decentralized network is built on a single open-source framework whose dominant branch constitutes a single deployment philosophy.","If entropy is measured at the wrong level (e.g., code-level diversity exists but data layer is monoculture)."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The total quantity of redundancy is irrelevant if it is monoculture; the large service's 'strictly more total internal redundancy' is rendered fragile by a single shared deployment philosophy. Independence is a distribution over causes, not a count of instances.","optional_refs":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672","bfed744d0afbc4ac27a1ccf2af5d4b0421ba4b9b479dd67eb4330769c450d5e3","4415b1db09640cd19341eacd8809fdc7f285bb19da86f8fa95273b88836a399e"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.81},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A decentralized network of small, independently operated services is often *less* resilient to correlated failure than one large service with strictly more total internal redundancy, due to premature exhaustion of recovery resources under cascading loads.","counterconditions":["If the large service's internal redundancy is concentrated in a monoculture, then the decentralized network is more resilient.","If there is a central, well-provisioned load-shedding circuit breaker governing the decentralized network's routing layer.","If the shocks are truly independent (not common-cause traffic shocks)."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Independently operated small services often lack backstop resources. A common-cause shock (e.g., a traffic spike or cache stampede) that a large service's redundant pools absorb can trigger simultaneous outages across many small services, meaning the observed joint failure distribution is dominated by correlated causes hidden as independent failures.","optional_refs":["eb4515296f5292fab30875a85afa5f21db8fbdea9615468bb2d07d0cc19c62e2","74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.32},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The distinction between failures that are truly independent and failures that merely appear uncorrelated until a shared cause is found is an artifact of the grain size of the causal model. For any apparently independent failure set, tracing back one level reveals a shared cause; for any apparently correlated failure set, decomposing one level reveals independent mechanisms. Therefore, the distinction is not a fundamental property of the network, but a model-relative projection.","counterconditions":["If there is a theoretically justified, canonical, and universal level of abstraction (a 'covering principle') that serves as the correct stopping point for causal regressions.","If one can empirically measure absolute causal primitives without any model-relative abstraction."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"There is no view-from-nowhere on independence. If two nodes fail at the same time, a deeper model posits a common cause; if one node fails, a deeper model decomposes its subsystems into independently failing parts. The requested evidence is thus epistemically incoherent because it assumes a privileged stopping point in the causal regression.","optional_refs":["8929789f0ae19d5b1c2537f758719daf17f9908ce1fb0ca3fa0cc60fe9f2a2a3"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.12},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The comparative resilience of the two architectures hinges on a single covering principle: the independence of redundancy is constrained by the physical and operational topology of its provisioning, not by its management structure. A decentralized network is more resilient if and only if its operator diversity is a reliable proxy for hardware, power grid, and network provider diversity.","counterconditions":["If small operators tend to share the same cloud provider or CDN at a rate higher than a large service uses multiple providers.","If the large service's 'strictly more total internal redundancy' is confined to a single physical region.","If large service organizational structure permits physical separation but mandates uniform hardware/software stacks, introducing a correlated software failure point that decentralization avoids."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"True independence requires physical independence. Centralizing management in a large service allows for strict, globally enforced physical separation (e.g., multi-region, multi-provider) which small, independently operated services may fail to achieve due to lack of expertise or budget. We unify both cases by looking at the topological independence of resources.","optional_refs":["30be11905db881ea7a4e488a8e68f34e4f6b262380bb3b1a31ceb9763a9aea81","ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.67},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The claimed independence of failures in a decentralized network is almost always an illusion masking hidden common dependencies, because the network itself enforces tight coupling via shared protocols and routing standards. The mere fact that small services can communicate implies a shared consensus mechanism that constitutes a correlated failure point.","counterconditions":["If the decentralized network uses multiple, mutually non-interoperable protocols that are partitioned into non-communicating subnets (reducing overall network value).","If the large service's internal subsystems share a single internal communication standard.","If the shared communication standard is formally verified to a degree that renders correlated failure probability negligible."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"All participants in a functioning decentralized network must agree on a shared communication protocol and routing logic. A bug or zero-day in the standard library implementing this protocol (e.g., OpenSSL, DNS) will simultaneously compromise all independent nodes. Therefore, one large service with diverse, non-communicating internal sub-systems may have lower correlation.","optional_refs":["4415b1db09640cd19341eacd8809fdc7f285bb19da86f8fa95273b88836a399e","74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.48},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Evidence distinguishing truly independent failures from merely uncorrelated ones can be gathered by subjecting the system to targeted causal perturbations rather than passively observing the joint failure distribution. If deliberately severing an operator-level factor that was thought to be shared fails to alter the joint failure rate, the independence is real; if it alters it, the independence was illusory.","counterconditions":["If the large service cannot be ethically or operationally subjected to randomization of its critical deployment philosophies.","If the act of randomization itself introduces new correlated failure modes (e.g., added orchestration complexity).","If there are unobserved operator-level factors that are not amenable to perturbation."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Passive observation (as in checking the joint failure distribution against operator factors) struggles with confounding and sparse data. The empiric covering principle is active intervention: active randomization of deployment philosophies or infrastructure vendors across the network breaks confounding and measures true causal independence.","optional_refs":["645d2e72e97c3e0836a1e98771a3765fc4b0a3f4b3d26e305d51006d38c89614","8929789f0ae19d5b1c2537f758719daf17f9908ce1fb0ca3fa0cc60fe9f2a2a3"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.55}],"simulation_proposals":[]}